For two resistors in parallel,
\[
R_{\mathrm{eq}}=\frac{R_1R_2}{R_1+R_2}
=\frac{(6.0)(3.0)}{6.0+3.0}
=2.0\,\Omega
\]

It decreases. Adding a positive branch adds a positive term to the conductance:
\[
\frac{1}{R'_{\mathrm{eq}}}
=\frac{1}{R_{\mathrm{eq}}}+\frac{1}{R_{\mathrm{new}}}
\]
Thus \(1/R'_{\mathrm{eq}}\) is larger, so \(R'_{\mathrm{eq}}\) is smaller.

Let the unknown resistor be \(R\):
\[
\frac{1}{4.0}=\frac{1}{12}+\frac{1}{R}
\]
\[
\frac{1}{R}=\frac{1}{4.0}-\frac{1}{12}
=\frac{3-1}{12}
=\frac{1}{6}
\]
So
\[
R=6.0\,\Omega
\]
